@section Mutation and ownership

This section covers the language's mutable cell, aliasing, and identity.
TypeScript has neither Rust's ownership rules nor a separate mutable-cell
type: the mutable cell is a @code{let}, and the interesting questions are
when this book permits one and what it costs.

@heading The captured @code{let}

A @code{let} declared in a function body is one mutable cell, and a
function that mentions it closes over the cell itself, not a copy. Two
closures made by the same call share one cell; two calls make two cells.
That is exactly the mechanism section @ref{3.1} exists to teach, which is
why this edition writes it with a plain @code{let} rather than hiding the
cell inside a class:

@example typescript
const makeCounter = (start: number): (() => number) => @{
  let n = start;
  return () => @{
    n += 1;
    return n;
  @};
@};

const c1 = makeCounter(0);
const c2 = makeCounter(0);

c1()
// => 1

c1()
// => 2

c2()
// => 1
@end example

@example typescript
const makeWithdrawer = (balance: number): ((amount: number) => number) => @{
  let b = balance;
  return (amount) => @{
    b -= amount;
    return b;
  @};
@};

const w = makeWithdrawer(100);

w(30)
// => 70

w(20)
// => 50
@end example

@noindent
The house rule follows from this: a @code{const} everywhere is the
default, and a captured @code{let} appears only where the book itself
assigns, which in this edition means the closure state of @ref{3.1} and
@ref{3.2}, the simulator internals of chapters 4 and 5, and the one table
and queue implementation of @ref{3.3}. Everywhere else the listings are
assignment-free. From @ref{3.1} the same assignment also becomes visible
in the type of the code: @code{Effect} and its @code{Ref} enter under
their own names, and a function that reads or writes a @code{Ref} says so
in its signature. This primer stays below that line; the cells here are
plain @code{let}.

@heading Aliasing and identity

Mutating one object is visible through every name that holds it. An
object lives once, and @code{const} freezes the binding, not the object,
so a non-readonly field changes under every alias:

@example typescript
interface Cell @{
  value: number;
@}

const cell: Cell = @{ value: 1 @};
const alias = cell;

cell.value = 9;

alias.value
// => 9

alias === cell
// => true
@end example

@noindent
This is the price and the point of chapter 3: two names for one mutable
object behave as one object. The interpreted pairs of @ref{3.3.1} are
built exactly here, a pair whose fields may be assigned, and
@code{eq?} on pairs is @code{===}, so identity is the question the book
asks of them. The persistent structures of @ref{0.4} exist precisely so
that the rest of the book never has to reason about such sharing.

Ownership, in the sense of the Rust edition, has no TypeScript
counterpart: the collector reclaims unreachable objects, and the book's
aliasing arguments are made with @code{===}, not with lifetimes. The
runnable version of this section's listings is
@file{packages/ch0/src/05-mutation.ts}, asserted by the test file beside
it.

